Assume Countable Choice. Fix the particle count $N\ge2$, real weights $\Gamma_i$, and $\varepsilon>0$. Let $H_\varepsilon(q,p)=\frac12|p|^2+V_\varepsilon(q)$ be the separable Hamiltonian of [[def-separable-regularized-vortex-interaction-hamiltonian]]. On an open collision-free configuration set, restrict the leapfrog step to states whose kick and drift stages remain in that set, as in [[def-local-leapfrog-domain-on-an-open-configuration-set]]. Then every admitted step has the following properties:

1. it is reversible: its inverse on its image is the admitted step with signed step size $-h$;
2. it is symplectic on its open admitted domain;
3. it preserves local phase volume, meaning Lebesgue measure of every measurable set and its image agree; and
4. for a fixed $T>0$ and integer $n\ge1$, with $h=T/n>0$ sufficiently small and exact initialization, if $V_\varepsilon\in C^3$ and the exact path, numerical states, every kick/drift stage, and every line segment required by [[thm-local-symplectic-euler-and-leapfrog-trajectory-errors]] lie in a common compact convex tube $K\Subset Q_{cf}\times(\mathbb R^2)^N$ independent of $n$, with derivatives of the Hamiltonian vector field through order two bounded there, the global trajectory error is $O(h^2)$ on $[0,T]$.

Claims 1–3 concern every signed admitted step; claim 4 has the stated positive-step tube hypotheses. The symplecticity and order-two conclusions use the exact hypotheses of the suppliers [[prop-local-leapfrog-and-symplectic-euler-are-symplectic]] and [[thm-local-symplectic-euler-and-leapfrog-trajectory-errors]]. This canonical second-order Hamiltonian is a comparison model; it is not the first-order Biot-Savart particle system.
